  "detail": "Thank you: inline links now resolve to their destinations, and 24 of the 25 destination tests fail without this round.\n\nRemaining, reproduced:\n- Reference-style links still match by their labels: full (`[#3][target]`), collapsed, shortcut, nested brackets, and definitions with titles. With `target` naming a foreign issue, `Acceptance: [#3][target]` produced `ready_for_acceptance`. `[slice][target]` naming local issue 3 produced `unfiled`.\n- Autolinks reach bare-reference matching (~179). `<https://github.com/owner/repo/issues/3>` produced `unfiled`, and `<https://example.com/?ref=#3>` named local child 3.\n\nEach round has found another Markdown spelling, and the source is now 223 lines against #920's ~100. Stop parsing Markdown. Narrow the grammar to the one #920 states (`#N`):\n- A list item names a slice only when its text, after an optional `Acceptance:` prefix, starts with `#N` or `owner/repo#N`, followed by whitespace, punctuation or the end of the line.\n- That leading token is the item's only reference. Nothing later in the line counts.\n- Every other form names nothing: links of every kind, autolinks, bare URLs, code spans, escaped `\\#N`, and a `#N` after other text (`- Q2 claims (#3)`). Such a line is `unfiled`. An `Acceptance:` item in such a form names no acceptance slice, so the native-blocker fallback applies.\n- Remove the inline-link parser.\n- State the leading-reference rule in docs/ISSUES.md.\n\nTests: replace the destination matrix with leading-token cases (local `#N`, foreign `owner/repo#N`, owner/repo matched case-insensitively, the `Acceptance:` prefix). Show that each of these names nothing and leaves the line `unfiled`: an inline link, a reference link with its definition, a collapsed reference, a shortcut reference, an autolink, a bare issue URL, a code span, an escaped reference, and `#N` after other text. Keep the queue-to-CLI test."
 }
